== Motivation ==

BIP 110 documents the background and rationale for its temporary consensus rules.
That material is incorporated by reference and is not repeated here.
<ref name="bip110">[[bip-0110.mediawiki|BIP 110: Reduced Data Temporary Softfork]]</ref>

When BIP 110's mandatory-signalling rules took effect at block 961,632 on 2026-08-08, nearly all then-existing SHA256d miners deliberately produced blocks that violated those consensus rules.
Those blocks were invalid Bitcoin blocks.
By extending the invalid blocks instead of Bitcoin's valid chain, the miners attempted a hostile hard fork.
Because it lacked consensus, the attempt failed to change Bitcoin's rules and instead produced a separate chain:
the data-storage altcoin referred to here as "Spamcoin".

Spamcoin's operators and supporters subsequently began a coordinated propaganda and social-engineering campaign--a psyop--to convince users that their invalid chain was Bitcoin and that the valid Bitcoin chain had instead forked away.

Bitcoin produced blocks 961,632 and 961,633 but then nearly stalled.
Block production at the time implied a remaining Bitcoin hash rate near 0.15% of the pre-split total.
At that rate, reaching the next 2,016-block difficulty adjustment would have taken approximately 25 years.
The chain retained a SHA256d difficulty of approximately 127.48 trillion despite having only a small fraction of the hash rate that established it.

Continuing with SHA256d would leave Bitcoin dependent on the return of miners who had already chosen to mine Spamcoin's invalid chain.
It would also leave those miners able to redirect their ASICs back to Bitcoin and overwhelm the remaining chain at will.

SHA256d proof of work has also suffered algorithmic breaks.
ASICBoost showed that specialized hardware can avoid a substantial part of the computation that SHA256d proof of work is meant to require, by exploiting the structure of SHA256d and the block header.
Such a shortcut, once discovered, rewards whoever controls it rather than whoever performs the most honest work.
Multiple parties are actively investing significant research and development in further breaking SHA256d proof of work, with credible hope of success.
ASICBoost proves the concept.
<ref name="asicboost">[https://arxiv.org/abs/1604.00575 Timo Hanke: AsicBoost - A Speedup for Bitcoin Mining]</ref>

This specification changes Bitcoin's proof of work to BLAKE2b.
Existing SHA256d ASICs cannot mine the new proof of work, allowing a new miner set to restore chain progress and security.

Since the proof-of-work recovery already requires every node to upgrade, this specification also addresses long-standing header and mining-protocol problems that were previously impractical to fix:

* the transaction count is committed to properly address CVE-2017-12842<ref>[https://bitcoinops.org/en/topics/merkle-tree-vulnerabilities/ Bitcoin Optech: Merkle tree vulnerabilities and CVE-2017-12842]</ref>;
* hashing hardware can roll timestamps without learning the real block time;
* additional nonces reduce dependence on rebuilding other commitments;
* pools can prevent block withholding attacks;
* different hashing-hardware interfaces can use explicit input profiles;
* merge-mining commitments can be placed close to proof of work; and
* the activation block verifiably restores an association between block height and real-world time.

== Specification ==

=== Definitions and notation ===

<code>TaggedHash(tag, message)</code> is the tagged SHA256 construction defined by BIP 340.
<ref>[[bip-0340.mediawiki|BIP 340: Schnorr Signatures for secp256k1]]</ref>
The tags in this document are case-sensitive ASCII strings.

In this document, <code>BLAKE2b(message)</code> means unkeyed BLAKE2b with a 256-bit digest.

Except where this document explicitly gives an integer interpretation, fields and intermediate values are exact bit strings, which have no endianness.
Integer fields used as hashing inputs are encoded as unsigned little-endian integers of the field's bit length.

<code>zeroes(n)</code> means an <code>n</code>-bit string containing only zero bits.
Concatenation is written using <code>||</code>.

A block identifier is the 256-bit string whose bytes appear, in order, in the block's conventional hexadecimal form.

=== Deployment parameters ===

A deployment of this specification defines:

* <code>activation_height</code>;
* <code>activation_headline</code>, an exact byte string; and
* <code>activation_target_shift</code>, the one-time target multiplier exponent applied at the activation block.

At heights below <code>activation_height</code>, blocks use the historical header and SHA256d proof of work.

At <code>activation_height</code> and every height thereafter, blocks MUST use the version 2 header and BLAKE2b proof of work specified below.

==== Mainnet parameters ====

Mainnet uses:

<pre>
activation_height       = 961640
activation_headline     = "8-30 NYPost Deride And Conquer"
activation_target_shift = 22
</pre>

The mainnet headline is the 30-byte ASCII encoding of the quoted text, without quotes or terminator.

The final SHA256d block, at height 961,639, had compact target <code>0x1702353d</code>.
The resulting activation block target is compact <code>0x1a008d4f</code>, a difficulty of about 130 P expected hashes.

==== Testnet4 parameters ====

Testnet4 uses:

<pre>
activation_height       = 150308
activation_headline     = ""  # no headline requirement
activation_target_shift = undefined
</pre>

The activation block was mined more than 20 minutes after its parent, so testnet4's minimum-difficulty rule gave it the proof-of-work limit, compact <code>0x1d00ffff</code>, a difficulty of about 4.3 G expected hashes.
The activation target adjustment applies only to the activation block, so no value of <code>activation_target_shift</code> can affect testnet4, and none is defined.
In practice, testnet4 behaves as if it were 0:
later blocks take their targets from earlier blocks, so they inherit the unshifted SHA256d target of the first block in the retarget period, as both the minimum-difficulty rule and the BIP 94 retarget look there.

=== Version field ===

Bit 31 of the 32-bit block version selects the version 2 header format.
This bit is part of the version field and is committed by the proof of work.

At heights below <code>activation_height</code>, bit 31 MUST be clear.
At and after <code>activation_height</code>, bit 31 MUST be set.

Versions <code>0x80000000</code> through <code>0x80000003</code> are invalid (see the [[#invalid-low-versions|corresponding lesson learned]]).

Version bits signalling, including mandatory signalling, uses top bits (bits 31 through 29) of <code>101</code> instead of <code>001</code> for version 2 blocks.

=== Version 2 block header fields ===

When version bit 31 is set, the block header consists of the fields below.
This section defines their widths and meanings, not their order, offsets, or padding in the serialized header;
the [[bip-blake2b-servicebit.mediawiki|peer-services specification]] defines that serialization.

The historical header fields remain, with the nonce extended:

{| class="wikitable"
! Bit length
! Field
! Meaning
|-
| 32 || Version
| Block version; bit 31 is set (see [[#Version_field|Version field]])
|-
| 256 || Previous block
| Identifier of the previous block
|-
| 256 || Merkle root
| Root of the merkle tree of the block's transactions
|-
| 32 || Timestamp
| Unsigned integer; see [[#Block_time|Block time]]
|-
| 32 || Target (<code>nBits</code>)
| Proof-of-work target, in Bitcoin's compact format
|-
| 96 || Nonce
| Mining nonce, extended from 32 bits
|}

The following existing block data is now also part of the header:

{| class="wikitable"
! Bit length
! Field
! Meaning
|-
| 31 || Height
| Height of this block
|-
| 16 || Transaction count
| Number of transactions in the block
|-
| 128 || Extranonce
| Extra nonce space, previously placed in the coinbase; input to the first BLAKE2b pass
|}

The following fields are new:

{| class="wikitable"
! Bit length
! Field
! Meaning
|-
| 1 || Time-offset enable bit
| Enable the time offset when determining block time
|-
| 32 || Time offset
| Unsigned integer; see [[#Block_time|Block time]]
|-
| 2 || Hashing-hardware profile selector
| Selects the hashing-hardware profile
|-
| 3 || Reserved bits
| Reserved for future use; SHOULD be zero, but do not affect validity, and validators MUST NOT assume they are zero
|-
| 8 || XOR-mask clear-bit count
| Integer from 0 through 255
|-
| 128 || XOR key
| Pool-selected key
|-
| 256 || Merge-mining right-hand side
| Undefined
|}

The nonce and the extranonce are inputs to different BLAKE2b passes.
The nonce is input only to the final BLAKE2b pass, so varying it requires no other recomputation.
The extranonce is input to the [[#First_BLAKE2b_pass|first BLAKE2b pass]], so varying it changes the intermediate digest supplied to hashing hardware.

A further protocol upgrade will need to extend the block time before it exceeds 2<sup>32</sup> - 1, in AD 2106;
the [[#Header_commitment_1|reserved time-extension field]] allows a 40-bit block time to be committed without changing the layout.
One will likewise need to change the height serialization before block height 2<sup>31</sup>, which, projecting forward from the present height at the ten-minute target spacing, would be reached around AD 42,838.

=== Block time ===

If the time-offset enable bit is clear, the block time used by all other consensus rules is the timestamp.

If the time-offset enable bit is set, the block time used by all other consensus rules is:

<pre>
(timestamp + time_offset) modulo 2**32
</pre>

The sum wraps, so the time offset does not extend the block-time range beyond AD 2106.

This definition applies to median-time-past, future-time, difficulty, locktime, and every other consensus rule that uses the block time.

The time-offset field is intended for timestamp rolling by hashing hardware.

=== Additional validity rules ===

For every version 2 block:

# The committed height MUST equal the actual block height.
# The proof-of-work digest MUST be computed as specified below and satisfy the target field.

Existing compact-target range, timestamp, transaction-validity, and block-weight rules continue to apply except as explicitly modified by this specification.
A version 2 block weighs 336 weight units more than a historical block with the same transactions;
this specification does not increase the block-weight limit, nor introduce a special header discount.

=== Proof-of-work overview ===

In summary, the proof-of-work digest is computed as follows;
the sections below define each step, including the encoding of each input:

<pre>
xor_key_hash = TaggedHash("Bitcoin block hash PoW XOR key", xor_key)

commitment_1 = TaggedHash("Bitcoin block header 1",
                   version || previous_block || height || merkle_root ||
                   timestamp || zeroes(8) || target ||
                   transaction_count || zeroes(16) || packed_bits ||
                   clear_bit_count || xor_key_hash)

hook         = TaggedHash("Merge-mining hook",
                   commitment_1 || zeroes(256) || mm_rhs)

intermediate = BLAKE2b(zeroes(32) || hook || extranonce)

final        = BLAKE2b(profile_input(intermediate, hook, nonce, time_offset,
                                     previous_block))

pow_digest   = final XOR mask(xor_key, clear_bit_count)
</pre>

where <code>packed_bits</code> holds the profile selector, time-offset enable bit, and reserved bits as defined under [[#Header_commitment_1|Header commitment 1]];
<code>profile_input</code> arranges its arguments according to the selected [[#Hashing-hardware_interface|hashing-hardware profile]];
and <code>mask</code> is the [[#Final_XOR_mask_and_proof-of-work_value|XOR mask]].

=== Proof-of-work commitments ===

Each field below is encoded as specified under [[#Definitions_and_notation|Definitions and notation]].

==== XOR-key hash ====

The XOR-key hash is:

<pre>
TaggedHash("Bitcoin block hash PoW XOR key", 128-bit XOR key)
</pre>

==== Header commitment 1 ====

The input to the tagged hash is the concatenation of these rows in order:

{| class="wikitable"
! Bit length
! Input
! Transformation
|-
| 32 || Version
| Little-endian, including bit 31
|-
| 256 || Previous block
| The previous block's identifier, in its natural byte order
|-
| 32 || Height
| 32-bit little-endian, whose most significant bit is reserved and zero
|-
| 256 || Merkle root
| Bit string
|-
| 32 || Timestamp
| Little-endian
|-
| 8 || Reserved time-extension field
| Zero
|-
| 32 || Target
| Compact target, encoded as in the historical header
|-
| 16 || Transaction count
| Little-endian
|-
| 16 || Reserved transaction-count extension field
| Zero
|-
| 8 || Profile selector, time-offset enable bit, and reserved bits
| From least significant: profile selector in bits 0-1, time-offset enable bit in bit 2, reserved bits in bits 3-5, and zero in bits 6-7
|-
| 8 || XOR-mask clear-bit count
| Integer
|-
| 256 || XOR-key hash
| Tagged-hash output defined above
|}

The concatenation is 952 bits (119 bytes).
Header commitment 1 is:

<pre>
TaggedHash("Bitcoin block header 1", concatenated_input)
</pre>

The zero bits following the timestamp allow a possible future 40-bit block time to be committed without changing this layout.
They do not currently extend the block-time range.

Likewise, the reserved transaction-count extension field allows a possible future expansion of the committed transaction count.

==== Merge-mining hook ====

The "right-hand side" is the header's 256-bit merge-mining field, named for its position to the right of header commitment 1 and the zero padding in the hook input.
The field is intended for Bitcoin's future expansion beyond a single blockchain;
this specification leaves its contents undefined.

The input to the tagged hash is:

{| class="wikitable"
! Bit length
! Input
|-
| 256
| Header commitment 1
|-
| 256
| Zero
|-
| 256
| Merge-mining right-hand-side bits from the header
|}

The merge-mining hook is:

<pre>
TaggedHash("Merge-mining hook", concatenated_input)
</pre>

The hook ties the merge-mining right-hand side to header commitment 1.

==== First BLAKE2b pass ====

The first BLAKE2b input is:

{| class="wikitable"
! Bit length
! Input
|-
| 32
| Zero
|-
| 256
| Merge-mining hook
|-
| 128
| Extranonce
|}

Hash the 416-bit (52-byte) concatenation with BLAKE2b.
Its 256-bit output is the intermediate digest.

=== Hashing-hardware interface ===

Hashing hardware receives a profile-specific final-pass input.
It does not receive the timestamp, target, time-offset enable bit, real block time, nor the unhashed previous-block field.

Hashing hardware chooses the time offset, advancing it as real time elapses, and returns it with its result.
Because the time-offset enable bit and timestamp are hidden in header commitment 1, hashing hardware cannot determine whether the time offset is applied, nor derive the real block time.

The hashing-hardware profile selector selects one of the following layouts.
The final BLAKE2b pass hashes the selected layout;
its 256-bit output is the final BLAKE2b digest.
In profiles 0, 2, and 3 the time offset is placed between the first 64 bits and the last 32 bits of the nonce.

==== Profile 0 ====

Profile 0 is intended for compatibility with [[#Existing_BLAKE2b_mining_hardware|existing BLAKE2b mining hardware]].

It uses a hidden previous-block value:

<pre>
TaggedHash("Bitcoin prevblock header, hashed", previous block identifier)
</pre>

with its first 48 bits then replaced with zeroes.

{| class="wikitable"
! Bit offset
! Bit length
! Input
|-
| 0 || 256 || Hidden previous-block value
|-
| 256 || 64 || First 64 bits of the nonce
|-
| 320 || 32 || Time offset
|-
| 352 || 32 || Last 32 bits of the nonce
|-
| 384 || 256 || Intermediate digest
|}

The total input length is 640 bits (80 bytes).

