=== Final XOR mask and proof-of-work value ===

If every bit of the 128-bit XOR key is zero, the XOR mask is 256 zero bits.

Otherwise, the initial XOR mask is:

<pre>
TaggedHash("Bitcoin block hash PoW XOR mask", 128-bit XOR key)
</pre>

Clear the requested number of bits from the start of the mask.
Bits are counted from the most significant bit of the first byte, then toward the least significant bit, and then through subsequent bytes.

The 256-bit proof-of-work digest is the bitwise XOR of the final BLAKE2b digest and the mask.

All inputs and intermediate values up to this point are bit strings.
Only now is a value interpreted as a number:
interpret the proof-of-work digest as a 256-bit big-endian unsigned integer and compare it with the target represented by the compact target field.

The block satisfies proof of work when that integer is less than or equal to the target.
The conventional hexadecimal block identifier is the proof-of-work digest written in byte order, without reversal.

=== Activation target adjustment ===

Without an adjustment, the first BLAKE2b block, at <code>activation_height</code>, would carry over the target from SHA256d mining, which is [[#Why_adjust_the_target_at_activation|not a measure of BLAKE2b work]].
That block alone instead uses a starting target for BLAKE2b mining, derived by scaling that target by a deployment-specific factor of 2<sup>activation_target_shift</sup>.

First calculate <code>sha256d_target</code>, the target the activation block would have under the SHA256d difficulty rules.

Decode that compact target to an integer, multiply it by 2<sup>activation_target_shift</sup>, and cap the result at the network proof-of-work limit:

<pre>
activation_target =
    min(proof_of_work_limit,
        sha256d_target * 2**activation_target_shift)
</pre>

Re-encode the result using Bitcoin's compact target format.
This is not merely serialization:
the compact format truncates low-order bits the result may have, and the truncated value is the activation block's target.

This additional adjustment is applied exactly once, at <code>activation_height</code>.
Later blocks use the ordinary difficulty rules without another algorithm-change adjustment.

The first ordinary retarget after activation measures the timespan of a period that began before activation.
If SHA256d block production slowed before activation, as it did on mainnet, that timespan is long, and the retarget makes the target easier, by up to the ordinary factor-of-four limit, regardless of how quickly blocks are found after activation.
This is expected, and no special rule applies to it.

=== Activation headline ===

At <code>activation_height</code>, the coinbase MUST contain <code>activation_headline</code> as a contiguous sequence of bytes, at any position.
The rule applies only to the activation block.

The required byte sequence MUST match exactly;
encoding and whitespace are part of the requirement.

== Rationale ==

'''Why does this upgrade change so much besides the proof-of-work algorithm?'''

The block header had to change in any case.
Existing BLAKE2b mining hardware cannot take the historical 80-byte header as input:
it expects a different input layout, with a larger nonce in a different position.
The historical 32-bit nonce was also too small for modern hardware regardless.

The other version 2 header features address long-standing problems and limitations.
They were not newly discovered while designing this specification;
they are included now because changing them required changing the block header, which this upgrade had to do anyway.

'''How does this specification keep mining from re-centralizing?'''

Replacing the proof of work recovers Bitcoin from SHA256d mining centralization, and the replacement itself, along with several other elements of this specification, also discourages the new miner set from re-centralizing.
These elements are directed specifically at keeping mining permissionless and competitive, and are collected here;
each is detailed in its own answer below.

* '''Precedent.'''<!--
--> This upgrade establishes that centralized mining can result in a proof-of-work change that strands the centralized hardware.<!--
--> That precedent is itself a strong disincentive against re-centralizing.
* '''Open hardware competition.'''<!--
--> BLAKE2b already has ASICs from [[#Existing_BLAKE2b_mining_hardware|several independent manufacturers]], so no first manufacturer starts with a monopoly head start, and no interim window exists for botnet dominance.<!--
--> BLAKE2b is also simple and cheap to implement in hardware:<!--
--> its small add-rotate-xor core can be laid out by any competent ASIC team, so new manufacturers can enter without the accumulated proprietary know-how of the SHA256d incumbents.
* '''Resistance to algorithmic breaks.'''<!--
--> No optimization shortcut analogous to overt or covert ASICBoost is known for BLAKE2b, and one is unlikely to exist:<!--
--> ASICBoost exploits SHA256's message expansion, whereas BLAKE2b has none, using every message word directly in every round.<!--
--> Such a shortcut raises the barrier to entry not only through patents or trade secrets, but also through the added complexity a new manufacturer must implement to compete.
* '''No advance knowledge.''' The algorithm was chosen by a [[#Selection_lottery|public deterministic lottery]] over a short list of viable candidates, so nobody could prepare specialized hardware before the result was public and start with a decisive fleet advantage.
* '''Header fixes.''' Since the header had to be reorganized anyway, several long-standing issues with it were fixed at the same time, including:
** '''Technical defence against block withholding.'''<!--
--> Previously, pools could defend against block withholding only through incentives and by identifying their customers (KYC).<!--
--> The XOR-key mechanism makes a complete technical defence possible.<!--
--> Pools therefore do not need customer identification to retain recourse against saboteurs;<!--
--> pooling stays open, competitive, and jurisdiction-independent instead of consolidating into a few permissioned providers.
** '''Hashing hardware isolated from Bitcoin internals.'''<!--
--> The 96-bit nonce gives hashing hardware ample search space, and the 128-bit extranonce does the same for the software that feeds it.<!--
--> The historical 32-bit nonce was too small, so mining protocols were forced to bring the coinbase transaction and merkle tree into scope for mining hardware.<!--
--> Hardware often handled those Bitcoin internals incorrectly:<!--
--> some could not handle larger coinbase transactions, which interferes with paying miners directly from the coinbase, and transferring them wasted bandwidth.
* '''Vendor-independent hardware lifetime.''' Hiding real time from hashing hardware (the hidden timestamp and hidden previous-block value) prevents an ASIC vendor from building reliable expiry "time bombs" into its products, which would let an incumbent vendor force refleeting on its own schedule and undermine second-hand markets that keep small miners viable.

'''Why not keep SHA256d?'''

SHA256d was unsuitable for the recovery because the hostile miner set already controlled an overwhelming amount of specialized SHA256d hardware.
It has also suffered algorithmic breaks such as ASICBoost.

'''Why BLAKE2b?'''

BLAKE2b was selected from a short list of viable candidates by the process described under [[#Algorithm_selection|Algorithm selection]].

BLAKE2b is a mature, extensively analyzed hash function with high performance on ordinary 64-bit CPUs (for easy verification by nodes), widely available implementations, and a simple unkeyed interface suitable for the required proof-of-work construction.

Its add-rotate-xor core also lends itself to compact, straightforward ASIC implementations, lowering the barrier for new hardware manufacturers.
No shortcut comparable to ASICBoost is known for BLAKE2b;
SHA256d's ASICBoost demonstrated how such a shortcut, once discovered, can distort and concentrate hardware manufacturing.

<span id="Existing_BLAKE2b_mining_hardware"></span> '''What BLAKE2b mining hardware already exists?'''

Mining hardware for BLAKE2b already existed before activation, from eight independent manufacturers:

* Baikal Giant B
* Bitmain Antminer A3
* Goldshell HS series
* Goldshell KD5 Pro (official BLAKE2b support); not all other KD-series machines are compatible, but some use the same chip and can be adapted with modified firmware
* Goldshell SC series
* Halong Mining DragonMint B52
* iBeLink BM-S series
* Innosilicon S11 SiaMaster
* Obelisk SC1 series
* StrongU STU-U2

Profile 0 is designed to be compatible with this hardware.

'''Why commit to the transaction count?'''

The transaction-count commitment properly addresses CVE-2017-12842, in which a pair of internal merkle-tree nodes and a 64-byte transaction could be mistaken for each other.
The merkle root by itself does not commit unambiguously to the number of leaves, so a malformed block body can share a header with a valid body that has a different transaction count.
Existing implementations work around the ambiguity, for example by treating some blocks containing 64-byte transactions as mutated, but then cannot cache such blocks as invalid, and must revalidate them each time they are seen.
Committing the count gives those bodies different headers, so an invalid body can be cached as invalid without risk of poisoning the cache against a valid one.

'''Are 16 bits enough for the transaction count?'''

The 16-bit committed transaction count can represent at most 65,535 transactions.
Under the general 4,000,000-weight-unit block limit, a version 2 block cannot contain more than 16,663 transactions even if every non-coinbase transaction has the minimum 60-byte stripped serialization.
Witness data can only reduce that transaction count.
The 16-bit width therefore does not constrain the general maximum, nor any lower block-weight limit imposed by other rules.

'''Why add a time offset?'''

The 96-bit nonce gives hashing hardware ample search space without the timestamp.
The time offset lets hardware that keeps working on the same input advance the block time as real time passes, so block timestamps stay accurate.
Doing so does not require knowing the real block time.

'''Why hide the timestamp from hashing hardware?'''

The timestamp is hidden inside header commitment 1 before hashing hardware receives its work.
The hardware chooses the offset, but cannot derive the real block time without the hidden timestamp.
This prevents an ASIC designer from building a reliable future-time "time bomb" that causes the hardware to stop working after a chosen real-world date.

'''Why hide the previous block identifier from hashing hardware?'''

Profile 0 includes a tagged hash of the previous block identifier rather than the identifier itself.
This prevents hashing hardware from using the visible previous-block hash to estimate Bitcoin's difficulty and therefore approximately infer real time.

The hidden value still occupies the position where existing BLAKE2b hardware expects a previous-block hash, and is derived only from the previous block identifier.
It therefore changes exactly when prior work becomes stale, in case any existing hardware relies on that field to detect new blocks.

'''Why are the first 48 bits of the hidden previous-block value zero?'''

The first 48 bits are always replaced with zeroes so existing BLAKE2b hardware can continue assuming that portion of its input is zero.

'''Why commit to a dedicated merge-mining field?'''

Committing a dedicated merge-mining field near proof of work allows a merge-mined sidechain to prove the commitment without carrying an entire merkle branch from an L1 generation transaction to the L1 block header.
This matters even though sidechain users already run an L1 full node:
a merge-mined block need only meet the sidechain's target, so it is not necessarily also an L1 block, and the L1 node may never see it.
The sidechain block must then carry its own proof of the commitment.

'''Why does the merge-mining hook input include 256 zero bits?'''

The zero bits put the entire merge-mining right-hand side, together with the SHA256 padding, in a separate final SHA256 input block:
the tag prefix of the merge-mining hook fills the first SHA256 input block, and the 256 zero bits after header commitment 1 complete the second.
A merge-mined chain can store the SHA256 midstate in place of header commitment 1, and compute the hook from its right-hand side with a single SHA256 compression rather than two (the tag's input block is the same for every hook, so its midstate is a constant).

'''Why does the first BLAKE2b input begin with 32 zero bits?'''

The 32 zero bits at the start of the first BLAKE2b input ensure that its first 8 bits are zero, as required by the legacy mining protocol implemented by the initial BLAKE2b mining firmware.
The field is 32 bits wide rather than 8 merely to align the following inputs somewhat.

'''Why have several hashing-hardware profiles?'''

The four profiles support different hashing-machine interfaces and fixed input sizes without changing commitments hidden from the machines.
The profile selector is itself committed, so changing profiles changes the proof-of-work digest.

* Profile 0 is designed to be compatible with existing BLAKE2b mining hardware.
* Profile 1 is a layout native to Bitcoin, intended for new hardware.

'''What is the XOR key for?'''

The XOR-key mechanism mitigates block-withholding attacks.
Until a share is returned, the hasher knows only the XOR key's hash, so it cannot tell whether a given share is also a valid block.
The key is committed in header commitment 1, while the configurable clear-bit count preserves the portion of the result needed for the intended mining interface.

'''Why does block withholding matter so much?'''

Block withholding is otherwise a pool's worst structural risk:
an attacker can submit shares for payment while discarding the winning blocks, and the pool's only deterrent is knowing who its customers are well enough to pursue them for the sabotage.
That pressure pushes pools toward mandatory customer identification and turns them into permissioned, jurisdiction-bound gatekeepers.
Because the hasher cannot recognize a winning block in order to withhold it, the attack becomes technically impossible rather than merely litigable, and a pool needs no customer identification to defend against it.

The detailed attack procedure is intentionally outside the scope of this document.

'''Wouldn't it be better to make pools impossible?'''

No.
Pooling over the network serves real needs, and preventing it would make mining more centralized, not less:

* '''Colocation.'''<!--
--> Miners who cannot pool over the network can still combine hash rate by moving their hardware into shared facilities under a single operator.<!--
--> That is strictly worse:<!--
--> the operator gains physical custody of the hardware as well as control of its work, and no proof-of-work rule can prevent it.
* '''Income variance.'''<!--
--> Miners have ongoing real costs, such as electricity, hosting, and financing, and many rely on consistent income to cover them.<!--
--> Without pooling, a small miner's income is a lottery with long dry spells, while a large operation finds blocks often enough for steady income, so larger miners would have a significant advantage.
* '''Trust favours incumbents.'''<!--
--> Proposals to make pools impossible, such as nonoutsourceable puzzles<!--
--><ref>[https://doi.org/10.1145/2810103.2813621 Andrew Miller, Ahmed Kosba, Jonathan Katz, Elaine Shi: Nonoutsourceable Scratch-Off Puzzles to Discourage Bitcoin Mining Coalitions]</ref><!--
-->, work by letting whoever finds a block take its entire reward.<!--
--> That deters only cooperation between parties who do not trust each other;<!--
--> a single large operation, or parties who already trust each other, can still combine hash rate.
* '''Pooling need not centralize block content.''' With getblocktemplate, or protocols built on it such as DATUM<ref>[https://convoy.xyz/docs/datum The Origins of DATUM]</ref>, each pooled miner can construct its own block, so a pool need only distribute rewards, not choose transactions.

'''Why commit to the block height?'''

The header provides the block's height directly for use by height-based rules, without requiring the coinbase transaction or a count of preceding blocks.
A node must still verify that the height is one greater than the previous block's height.

<span id="Why_adjust_the_target_at_activation"></span> '''Why adjust the target at activation?'''

SHA256d and BLAKE2b work are not commensurable, so the prior SHA256d target says nothing about how much BLAKE2b work a block should require.
The [[#Activation target adjustment|activation target adjustment]] therefore sets a starting target suited to initial BLAKE2b mining, once, after which the ordinary difficulty rules calibrate it to the actual BLAKE2b hash rate.

'''Why commit to a headline in the activation block?'''

While Bitcoin was nearly stalled, its abnormally long block interval broke the natural link between block height and real-world time.
The headline commitment restores that link in a verifiable way:
it proves that the first BLAKE2b block was not mined before the public restoration of Bitcoin on 2026-08-30.

'''Why this headline?'''

The mainnet headline is the front-page headline of the New York Post's print edition for 2026-08-30.
Mining with BLAKE2b began immediately when the New York Post published that front page online, so nobody could have mined the activation block before then.
The rule applies only to the activation block.

=== Lessons learned after the fact ===

The following observations were made after this design was deployed.
They are non-normative and do not change any rule above.

* <span id="invalid-low-versions"></span>The invalidity of versions <code>0x80000000</code> through <code>0x80000003</code> is a bug.<!--
--> The reference implementation applies the BIP 34, BIP 66, and BIP 65 minimum-version rules to the version with bit 31 removed, so these versions are rejected as if they were the obsolete versions 0 through 3.<!--
--> Because the reference implementation enforces it, the restriction is part of consensus;<!--
--> the rule is stated under [[#Version_field|Version field]].<!--
--> It has no practical effect, since a version 2 block has no reason to use these versions.
* Clearing one more leading bit of the hidden previous-block value (49 rather than 48) would have left a value, such as <code>0x80</code> in the byte following the zeroed bits, that profile 0 can never produce. A future hashing protocol could have used it to signal "no change" unambiguously.
* Profiles 1 through 3 include the merge-mining hook in the final pass so that a merge-mined chain's block headers could omit the extranonce and skip computing the intermediate digest.<!--
--> In practice this gains nothing:<!--
--> storing the 256-bit intermediate digest instead takes twice the space of the 128-bit extranonce.<!--
--> Had the final pass included the merge-mining right-hand side instead of the merge-mining hook, merge-mined chains could have omitted both header commitment 1 and the extranonce, saving 16 bytes.

== History ==

This section preserves the historical record of the upgrade.
It is non-normative.

=== Algorithm selection ===

This section preserves, for the historical record, how the replacement algorithm was selected.
The events took place in public community channels during August 2026.
Announcements and commitments were published in parallel on X/Twitter, on Nostr, and in the Bitcoin Knots Discord and Telegram, and the drawing announcement with its first commitment hash was also posted on Bluesky and Mastodon.
The events were covered by contemporary press reports.
The commitment texts, salts, and beacon observations below are preserved from the author's contemporaneous public posts.

==== Screening criteria ====

Candidate algorithms were screened against four criteria, which the author first proposed in the Bitcoin Knots Discord on 2026-08-09, two days before the 2026-08-11 drawing.
The goal was not ASIC resistance, nor keeping CPUs or GPUs competitive, but a proof of work that is secure from the first day.
The criteria were:

# '''An existing fleet of ASICs.'''<!--
--> Without one, the first manufacturer to produce hardware would gain a decisive advantage merely by arriving first, and Bitcoin would be dominated by malicious botnets in the interim.<!--
--> An existing fleet was valued for this reason, not for its size:<!--
--> a fleet so large, liquid, or hostile that it could immediately control or attack Bitcoin was itself disqualifying.<!--
--> Stock SHA256d, and algorithms whose existing fleet was large and hostile enough to overwhelm Bitcoin, were excluded on that ground.
# '''Open manufacturer entry.'''<!--
--> Once the ASIC market matures, new independent manufacturers must still be able to enter it easily.<!--
--> This is determined mainly by the complexity of the algorithm.<!--
--> SRAM dependency was also considered, but there were no good options requiring it;<!--
--> Scrypt, for example, was widely considered to have a hostile fleet.
# '''Break risk.'''<!--
--> The construction must be conservative and well understood, with low risk of another mathematical break of the kind ASICBoost inflicted on SHA256d.<!--
--> A newly invented primitive would have lacked the review and implementation maturity needed for an emergency recovery.
# '''Cheap verification.'''<!--
--> The fundamental principle of proof of work:<!--
--> verification must remain very easy even while mining is difficult.<!--
--> Invalid blocks must be rejected quickly and cheaply, starting with their headers;<!--
--> memory-hard designs for which an invalid header can force a full expensive computation fail this asymmetry and were excluded.

Consensus was also to be kept clean of legacy mining-protocol compatibility concerns.

==== Candidates ====

After height 961,633, Bitcoin's block production slowed nearly to a stall.
Replacement algorithms were debated publicly in Bitcoin Knots community channels beginning 2026-08-09.
Algorithms and approaches raised in that discussion included BLAKE2 variants, BLAKE3, KT256, RandomX, Scrypt, Autolykos v2, and generic CPU- or GPU-oriented mining.
The short list ultimately committed for the drawing comprised five algorithms, each with existing ASIC hardware:
BLAKE2b-256, BLAKE2s, BLAKE3x2, Eaglesong, and SHA3x.

On 2026-08-15, the author republished the [[#Screening_criteria|screening criteria]] more broadly, together with an assessment table covering roughly forty candidate algorithms, each rated for its existing fleet or bootstrap path, maturity of ASIC-market competition, hidden-optimization and PoW-function risk, and cheapness of invalid-header rejection.
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2088706321386262771 Author's post of the four screening criteria and candidate assessment table]</ref>
Judgments preserved from that table include:

* BLAKE2b-256: best overall balance of fleet, regular hardware, and confidence.
* SHA3x: very clean Keccak-family hardware, with fleet concentration as the main concern.
* KT256 and clean SHA3-256: "greenfield reserve" -- excellent constructions with no existing fleet for the exact variant, dominated for launch by SHA3x's related Keccak path with an existing fleet.
* Scrypt:<!--
--> unusually strong real-world evidence of repeated manufacturer entry, though more complex than a plain hash.<!--
--> It was nevertheless removed late in the process because many participants had substantiated fears that the existing Scrypt miner fleet would be hostile to Bitcoin.
* Stock SHA256d: excluded from the algorithm lottery outright -- ASICBoost history plus accumulated physical-design expertise, firmware advantages, and supply-chain entrenchment.
* kHeavyHash (the only real-world implementation of BIP 52 optical proof of work, for which no optical mining hardware actually exists): excluded for an enormous, liquid, and hostile fleet able to overwhelm Bitcoin.
* RandomX v2: excluded because a competitive ASIC would resemble a custom CPU.
* Meaningfully parameterized Argon2d: excluded because a small invalid header can force the full expensive memory-hard computation, an unacceptable denial-of-service asymmetry.

==== Selection lottery ====

Rather than allowing any individual or committee to pick the winner, the process adopted was to fix a short list of viable candidates and let a deterministic random drawing make the final selection.
As the announcement put it:
"Nobody knows the new algorithm right now, and nobody will until we all know at the same moment."
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087164051797192886 Drawing announcement with the first proof-of-fairness commitment hash (13:07 UTC 2026-08-11)]</ref>
The candidate list was curated so that only acceptable options were on it;
as the author later summarized, "It's not important that it be truly random, only that nobody got advance notice of it."
<ref>[https://njump.me/note1hdu8qxj8v8urr4m8zgs5jjw9f6ljw62hnfjxzhclnkj8z6t9kwvsgyrx05 Author on the selection principle (Nostr)] and [https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087313112944750990 Similar statement on X]</ref>
Advance knowledge of the winning algorithm would have let its holder begin hardware preparation early.

===== Failed first drawing =====

The drawing was announced at 13:07 UTC on 2026-08-11.
Its randomness beacon was the last hexadecimal digit of the hash of the first testnet4 block found after 14:00 UTC that day.
The digit-to-candidate mapping was fixed in advance together with a random salt, but kept secret;
only its SHA256 hash (a "proof of fairness") was published with the announcement:
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087164051797192886 Drawing announcement on X (13:07 UTC)] and [https://njump.me/note187zecrjmqjcwewl6y6vjpxaeqc50w99ty7rrxktrxcq6c4lev53sv4jcgq Nostr post (13:09 UTC)]</ref>

  71a7960874f3352f4ba600ce67bd6f8e96702565abea93021fa86349d99a45e8

Concealing the mapping until after the beacon meant that even a party able to grind the beacon could not aim it at a specific algorithm;
the salt prevents brute-forcing the committed contents from the published hash.
As the announcement put it:
"You can prove I didn't rig it, by SHA256 hashing the mapping text file."

The mapping was revealed at 14:13 UTC, immediately after the beacon window:
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087180586653716646 Reveal of the committed digit-to-candidate mapping and salt (14:13 UTC)]</ref>

<pre>
0 SHA3x
1 BLAKE2s
2 BLAKE2s
3 BLAKE3x2
4 SHA3x
5 SHA3x
6 BLAKE2b-256
7 SHA3x
8 BLAKE2b-256
9 BLAKE2b-256
a BLAKE2s
b BLAKE3x2
c Eaglesong
d BLAKE3x2
e BLAKE2b-256
f Eaglesong

Random salt: 388d2e17730db6f15a34aa3f2a67808c
</pre>

The resulting weights were 4/16 each for SHA3x and BLAKE2b-256, 3/16 each for BLAKE2s and BLAKE3x2, and 2/16 for Eaglesong.

The first block observed after 14:00 UTC was testnet4 height 147,930, <code>0000000000db6829f71bee436279bdacd2af7d20a75e710da073f1ad42d65107</code>, whose final digit <code>7</code> mapped to SHA3x.
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087180796188594387 Observed first-draw beacon block at testnet4 height 147,930 (14:14 UTC)]</ref>
But three competing testnet4 blocks were mined simultaneously at the same height -- publicly attributed to hostile actors deliberately disrupting the drawing -- so there was no unambiguous "first block".
The competing blocks' digits selected different candidates, leaving SHA3x and BLAKE2b-256 in contention.

===== Superseded second commitment =====

A second commitment, now between only the two remaining candidates, was posted at 14:43 UTC as a bare SHA256 hash:
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087188103718973731 Second proof-of-fairness commitment hash (14:43 UTC)]</ref>

  b96b58acccb69746ea7b2f1b9793ed53cda2cdbe5fd7d5ef1878178bbef537f0

This time even the criteria themselves were hidden inside the commitment.
The first drawing's publicly pre-announced procedure had been attacked the moment it became actionable, so the author declined to describe the new procedure in advance:
"Apparently if I say [the criteria], it will get attacked.
The criteria is part of the new hash.
When I post it, it'll be clear."

The criteria -- revealed "for the record" at 19:53 UTC, after the selection had concluded, honoring the commit-then-reveal discipline even for a superseded stage -- were:
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087266122609430536 For-the-record reveal of the superseded second criteria (19:53 UTC)]</ref>

<pre>
Next Testnet4 block after 14:45 UTC that goes uncontested for 5 minutes. 0-7 is SHA3x, 8,9,a-f is BLAKE2b.
</pre>

The uncontested-for-five-minutes requirement was designed to fix the ambiguity failure of the first drawing, but set the bar too high:
no qualifying block appeared for over two hours.
Meanwhile, at 15:51 UTC, an override was announced publicly:
"Alternatively:
If a valid (including BIP110 of course) Bitcoin block is found prior to the new criteria being met, we can use that instead."
Testnet4 was acknowledged to be a poor beacon, and a valid Bitcoin block -- whose production cost makes grinding uneconomical -- was preferred wherever available;
but with mainnet finding roughly one block every other day at the post-split hash rate, it could not serve as the primary beacon, and the alternatives (trusting a third-party randomness source, or syncing a hostile altcoin's node) were ruled out.

===== Final draw =====

At 17:01 UTC the final criteria were committed, again as a bare SHA256 hash ("It's been an hour.
Clearly I set the bar too high.
I will reveal the previous criteria when we're done.
In the meantime, here is the new criteria"):<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087222841213083931 Final proof-of-fairness commitment hash (17:01 UTC)]</ref>

  ba712f48962a43163266cec09a069825b89a316140a9701fa4931b5fb6407f4e

The committed text, revealed at 17:04 UTC, was:
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087223637271052452 Reveal of the committed final-draw criteria and salt (17:04 UTC)]</ref>

<pre>
Take the final hex digit from all Testnet4 blocks found after 17:00 UTC, but stopping after 60 seconds of "silence" (no new blocks).

Sum those digits, and take the last digit of the sum.

0-7 = SHA3x
8,9,a-f = BLAKE2b

If a valid (BIP110) Bitcoin block is found prior to this condition being satisfied, the last digit of that block shall be used instead.

Random salt: e4ad05e245927a5c398d2a6d6816bf9a
</pre>

Four testnet4 blocks were observed between 17:02:11 and 17:02:16 UTC, followed by more than sixty seconds of silence, closing the window:
<ref>[https://x.com/LukeDashjr/status/2087224213199348166 Observed final-draw beacon blocks, testnet4 heights 147,949-147,952 (17:06 UTC)]</ref>

